<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kayqubad I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">died 1237</span> · Also known as Kaykubad I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kayqubad I was sultan of the Seljuk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> from 1220 to 1237, the period of its greatest power. He took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alanya">Alanya</a>, which he rebuilt as a winter capital, extended Seljuk rule in eastern Anatolia, absorbing the Mengujekid lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erzincan">Erzincan</a>, and built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravanserai">caravanserais</a>, palaces and fortresses across his realm. In 1230 he defeated the Khwarazmian ruler Jalal al-Din.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kayqubad-i.jpg" alt="Kayqubad I" width="320" height="310" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: American Numismatic Society · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ala%27_al-Din_Kay_Qubad_I,_Ankuriyya,_1219-1237.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
